The UI process's backing store in two buffers, in memory the WebKit Kit
makes (Kosmos webkit.md Fast, step 1c; KosmosStore.h). JetSki copied the
page from the store into its window every frame; now the window manager
is handed the store's region and shows the page from it, as an inset over
the window. So it is written into one buffer while the other is on the
screen: kosmosShow says the buffer just finished and what changed in it,
and starts the other, which first catches up - every scroll it missed in
one move in place, then the rectangles the first changed, copied across -
rather than the whole view each frame. Where there is no such memory, or
another scale than one, one buffer of its own, as before.

